		tags = { "Food types", "Meat and other animal products" },
		summary = "**Seafood** is any form of [[Marine life|sea life]] regarded as food by humans, prominently including [[Fish as food|fish]] and [[Shellfish|shellfish]]. Shellfish include various species of [[Mollusca|molluscs]] (e.g., bivalve molluscs such as clams, [[Oyster|oysters]], and mussels, and [[Cephalopod|cephalopods]] such as octopus and squid), [[Crustacean|crustaceans]] (e.g. shrimp, crabs, and lobster), and [[Echinoderm|echinoderms]] (e.g. sea cucumbers and sea urchins). Historically, [[Marine mammal|marine mammals]] such as [[Cetacean|cetaceans]] ([[Blue whale|whales]] and [[Dolphin|dolphins]]) as well as [[Pinniped|seals]] have been eaten as food, though that happens to a lesser extent in modern times. Edible sea plants such as some seaweeds and microalgae are widely eaten as sea vegetables around the world, especially in Asia.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Seafood is an important source of (animal) [[Protein|protein]] in many [[Diet (nutrition)|diets]] around the world, especially in coastal areas. Semi-vegetarians who consume seafood as the only source of meat are said to adhere to pescetarianism. The harvesting of wild seafood is usually known as [[Fishing|fishing]] or [[Hunting|hunting]], while the cultivation and farming of seafood is known as [[Aquaculture|aquaculture]] and fish farming (in the case of fish). Most of the seafood harvest is consumed by humans, but a significant proportion is used as fish food to farm other fish or rear farm animals. Some seafoods (i.e. [[Kelp|kelp]]) are used as food for other plants (a [[Fertilizer|fertilizer]]). In these ways, seafoods are used to produce further food for human consumption. Also, products such as fish oil, spirulina tablets, fish collagen, and [[Chitin|chitin]] are made from seafoods. Some seafood is fed to [[Aquarium|aquarium]] fish, or used to feed domestic pets such as [[Cat|cats]].",
